}
```

A track is one sequential line of work, so steps within it never overlap; concurrent work belongs on separate tracks. Each step has exactly one duration kind: fixed, variable, or indefinite, with the corresponding duration fields. A start trigger connects a step to the program clock, another step, a manual action, an abort, or a compound condition. Step IDs must be unique across the entire program, and every task used by a step needs a matching resource constraint whose `maxConcurrent` limits simultaneous use. Repeated work is represented once with `replicates`, never by copying steps or tracks; per-instance successors use `instances: "each"`, barriers use `instances: "all"`, and `maxInFlight` limits work held between those stages. For this recipe, I expect two burner slots, one shared prep capacity because there is one cook, and one working actor.

Expected resource constraints:

- `burner`: `maxConcurrent` 2 — the two available burners.
- `prep`: `maxConcurrent` 1 — one cook cannot perform two hands-on preparation tasks simultaneously.

